WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:06.210
In 1995, Katalin Karikó
had hit a low point.

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:09.120
A biochemist at the
University of Pennsylvania,

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:12.530
she'd devoted her entire
career to turning mRNA,

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:15.270
one of the most important
building blocks of life,

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:17.640
into a whole new field of medicine.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.860
But it just wasn't happening.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:21.830
Everybody argued that RNA

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:23.960
is so transient and degrades

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:27.320
and it will never be medicine.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:30.100
I knew that it can be used for everything

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:32.410
and, you know, kind of a Cassandra feeling

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:34.450
that I can see the future

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:35.873
and nobody believes me.

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:39.860
Karikó's grant applications were rejected.

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:42.160
She was demoted, endured a cancer scare,

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:44.170
but she stuck with it.

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:45.820
She spent weekends and holidays

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:47.830
doing research and experiments.

00:00:47.830 --> 00:00:51.210
And in 2005, Karikó and
her colleague Drew Weissman

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:53.160
published a breakthrough study

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:56.120
confirming the therapeutic use of mRNA,

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:58.260
research which would underpin the success

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:01.110
of mRNA Covid vaccines today.

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:03.820
When the first Covid-19
vaccine was approved,

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:05.610
I was extremely excited.

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:09.360
I thought it was a
changed moment for mRNA.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.000
What we've been seeing
in animals was real,

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.350
and it works in people.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:16.450
And that's the most important thing.

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:18.800
mRNA has gone from a rejected idea

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:22.160
to a powerful weapon
helping to end the pandemic.

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:24.110
And applications of this technology

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:26.593
could provide a cure for
diseases beyond Covid.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.630
Messenger RNA, or mRNA,

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:39.130
is a genetic material

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:42.320
that copies instructions from our DNA.

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:45.140
Our cells use these
instructions to make proteins,

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:46.830
which carry out different functions

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:48.250
in the human body.

00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:52.190
mRNAs are responsible for
everything our body does,

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:55.450
from breathing air to eating food,

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:59.180
to walking, to sleeping, to thinking.

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:01.440
Covid vaccines from Pfizer-BioNTech

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:04.870
and Moderna are based
on this mRNA mechanism.

00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:06.360
Unlike traditional vaccines

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:08.640
using dead or weakened viruses,

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:11.450
mRNA vaccines use the genetic information

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:12.800
of the virus.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:15.990
Scientists make an mRNA that
codes for the spike protein

00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:20.600
of SARS-CoV-2, the virus
that caused Covid-19.

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:23.470
Then the mRNA is put
inside a lipid coating

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:25.333
and injected into the human body.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:28.160
The cell reads the mRNA

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:31.200
and starts to make harmless
spike proteins of its own,

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:32.650
which triggers the immune system

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:35.163
to produce antibodies against the virus.

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.720
The way that mRNA vaccines work

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:41.490
is similar to really all vaccines.

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:44.520
What they're doing is they're presenting

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:48.500
a protein or proteins from a pathogen,

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:50.310
and they're causing our bodies

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:52.450
to make an immune response

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:55.280
against those proteins or protein.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:59.370
When you make an
inactivated virus vaccine,

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:02.090
you have to isolate the virus.

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:04.520
You have to grow it up in eggs.

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:07.540
You have to figure out
how to grow it in eggs.

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:10.870
You have to purify it and activate it,

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:13.000
and formulate it.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:16.290
With mRNA, you only need the sequence.

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:18.150
You don't need the virus.

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:21.413
So it's a very simple vaccine to make.

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:25.000
This means that making mRNA vaccines

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:27.203
is faster than making traditional ones.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:31.120
But the journey for mRNA to
break into mainstream medicine

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:32.753
has been long and challenging.

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:37.470
mRNA was first discovered in 1961.

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:40.390
But it wasn't until 1984
that scientists were able

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:41.993
to recreate it in the lab.

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:45.600
In the 1990s, as scientists knew more

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:47.680
about the make-up of human DNA,

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:49.380
gene therapy became popular,

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:51.980
inserting a gene into a patient's cells

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:53.623
to treat or prevent disease.

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:56.590
But most researchers gave little thought

00:03:56.590 --> 00:03:59.260
to using mRNA as a vaccine or a drug,

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:02.410
despite its close relationship with DNA.

00:04:02.410 --> 00:04:04.560
I argued that the messenger RNA

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:06.760
is more like a conventional drug.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:08.450
You can apply,

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:10.640
and then if you see a beneficial effect,

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:13.590
you can reapply, and
then the healing is done,

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:15.573
rather than a gene therapy

00:04:15.573 --> 00:04:17.583
where you are delivering the gene

00:04:17.583 --> 00:04:19.580
and you cannot control.

00:04:19.580 --> 00:04:21.740
But many rejected Karikó's idea,

00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:25.320
arguing mRNA therapies
didn't look feasible.

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:27.180
The immune system is hypervigilant

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:29.720
against foreign RNA entering the body,

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:33.370
and so the injected lab-made
RNA caused inflammation

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:35.070
which destroyed it before it could start

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:37.060
to trigger an immune response.

00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:39.200
And even worse, that inflammation

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:41.270
could be life-threatening.

00:04:41.270 --> 00:04:44.540
In 1997, Karikó met Drew Weissman,

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:46.300
a respected immunologist

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:48.480
and they started to collaborate.

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:51.500
The big discovery was we figured out

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:54.040
by modifying the nucleoside.

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:56.490
So RNA has four letters

00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:58.750
that code for a protein.

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:03.100
Each letter is a nucleoside or a base.

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:05.970
We modified those bases

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:09.543
and that reduced and got
rid of the inflammation.

00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:14.870
When we altered uridine
and we had a pseudouridine,

00:05:14.870 --> 00:05:19.120
we found that not only we have
an RNA which is non-immunogenic,

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:22.140
but we had a very high
level of protein production.

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:25.620
The amount of protein made from the RNA

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:28.830
increases about a thousand fold.

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:30.420
You get rid of inflammation

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:34.420
and you increase potency in the same step.

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:37.376
So it was like a dream come true.

00:05:37.376 --> 00:05:39.259
They published the key research

00:05:39.259 --> 00:05:41.210
and filed a patent,
which was later licensed

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:42.960
to Moderna and BioNTech,

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:46.520
the companies making mRNA
Covid vaccines today.

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:49.943
Karikó also started to
work for BioNTech in 2013.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:54.260
But there was another hurdle-

00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:57.890
how to deliver mRNA to targeted cells.

00:05:57.890 --> 00:06:00.230
mRNA is notoriously fragile,

00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:02.960
and enzymes in the human
body can break it down.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:06.060
So you really need to have it protected

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:09.562
from the enzymes and other
kinds of things in the body.

00:06:09.562 --> 00:06:11.640
You could call them the
bad guy, so to speak.

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:14.760
And if you don't, then the
therapy won't work at all,

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:16.520
it would be non-existent.

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:18.550
As early as the 1970s,

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:20.300
chemical engineer Robert Langer

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:22.050
and his colleague Judah Folkman

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:23.230
proved that it's possible

00:06:23.230 --> 00:06:25.370
to deliver molecules like RNA

00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:28.850
inside tiny particles
without destroying them.

00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:31.980
The nanoparticles Langer
used were made of polymers,

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:36.320
not lipids as used in
today's mRNA Covid vaccines.

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:39.060
So we were trying to put DNA and RNA

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:41.470
into tiny particles and deliver them.

00:06:41.470 --> 00:06:44.400
I failed the first 200
times when I did it.

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:47.390
And by the way, that was
also widely criticized.

00:06:47.390 --> 00:06:49.870
So I got my first nine grants turned down.

00:06:49.870 --> 00:06:52.880
I couldn't get a job,
you know, a faculty job

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:54.400
at the chemical engineering department,

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:56.270
which was my area.

00:06:56.270 --> 00:06:58.800
But today Langer is a renowned scientist

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:01.500
with over 900 issued patents.

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:03.780
In 2010, he co-founded Moderna

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:05.453
and still sits on its board.

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:09.580
After decades of development,

00:07:09.580 --> 00:07:12.270
the main biological
roadblocks were cleared,

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:14.680
but mRNA technology had never been used

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:17.223
outside of clinical trials until recently.

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:19.980
In the last few minutes we've heard

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:22.190
the first coronavirus vaccine

00:07:22.190 --> 00:07:24.560
has been approved for use in the UK.

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:26.010
This is being manufactured

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:28.610
by the U.S. pharmaceutical company Pfizer

00:07:28.610 --> 00:07:30.650
and its partner BioNTech.

00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:32.430
And here is the first person

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:34.123
in line to get it.

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:39.260
In the following months, mRNA vaccines

00:07:39.260 --> 00:07:41.360
from both Pfizer-BioNTech and Moderna

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:43.860
were cleared in multiple countries.

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:47.030
Taking less than a year from
the lab into people's arms,

00:07:47.030 --> 00:07:48.690
these are the fastest vaccines

00:07:48.690 --> 00:07:50.240
that have ever been developed,

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:52.773
both with a 95% efficacy rate.

00:07:53.750 --> 00:07:55.030
But some people are concerned

00:07:55.030 --> 00:07:56.923
that these vaccines are hastily made.

00:07:57.990 --> 00:08:01.150
The speed of making the vaccine,

00:08:01.150 --> 00:08:03.300
the 10 months that it took.

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:04.810
There's very good reasons

00:08:04.810 --> 00:08:07.840
that people need to
understand why that happened.

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:09.070
It was an emergency.

00:08:09.070 --> 00:08:10.680
It was a pandemic.

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:14.150
All of the clinical trials
were done at the same time.

00:08:14.150 --> 00:08:17.630
All of the testing was done together.

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:19.680
No corners were cut.

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:23.240
There were no safety
issues that were ignored.

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:25.720
Everything was done exactly the same

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:27.830
as every other vaccine.

00:08:27.830 --> 00:08:29.317
In December 2020,

00:08:29.317 --> 00:08:32.050
Karikó and Weissman were
given the Covid vaccines

00:08:32.050 --> 00:08:33.543
they had contributed to.

00:08:34.586 --> 00:08:38.122
People were waiting for
the healthcare workers

00:08:38.122 --> 00:08:41.068
in line there to get their vaccines.

00:08:41.068 --> 00:08:42.250
And, you know, they clapped.

00:08:42.250 --> 00:08:43.683
And then I cried.

00:08:45.250 --> 00:08:49.250
About 3.2 billion doses
of mRNA Covid vaccines

00:08:49.250 --> 00:08:51.813
are expected to be given in 2021.

00:08:52.690 --> 00:08:55.440
And now mRNA may also
be used to help fight

00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:57.610
future pandemics and diseases

00:08:57.610 --> 00:09:00.337
that current medicines struggle to treat.

00:09:00.337 --> 00:09:02.380
The mRNA is just an instruction

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:06.040
for the cell to make the protein.

00:09:06.040 --> 00:09:10.310
We can target the messenger
RNA to certain organs,

00:09:10.310 --> 00:09:13.170
certain cell types, bone marrow,

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:17.540
and then can perform
different kind of treatment.

00:09:17.540 --> 00:09:19.300
There are many different applications

00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:20.460
for messenger RNA.

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:22.800
I mean, you know, at Moderna,

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:24.270
they're working on heart disease,

00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:27.170
they're working on
personalized cancer vaccines.

00:09:27.170 --> 00:09:30.010
They're working on rare diseases.

00:09:30.010 --> 00:09:33.020
I think the next big act for RNA

00:09:33.020 --> 00:09:35.910
are probably gonna be more vaccines.

00:09:35.910 --> 00:09:37.830
Last summer, we started working

00:09:37.830 --> 00:09:40.890
on a pan-coronavirus vaccine.

00:09:40.890 --> 00:09:44.230
So there've been three
coronavirus epidemics

00:09:44.230 --> 00:09:47.670
and pandemics in the past 20 years.

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:50.420
You have to assume there's gonna be more.

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:53.330
Can we make a vaccine that prevents

00:09:53.330 --> 00:09:57.047
against all of those bat coronaviruses

00:09:57.047 --> 00:10:00.050
that have the potential to infect people

00:10:00.050 --> 00:10:03.120
and start another epidemic or pandemic?

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:07.090
I think that the pan-influenza
and pan-coronavirus

00:10:07.090 --> 00:10:10.300
are gonna be the most
exciting RNA vaccines

00:10:10.300 --> 00:10:12.380
to come out in the future.

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:15.990
mRNA has been proven now
as a really impressive

00:10:15.990 --> 00:10:18.360
and powerful new technology,

00:10:18.360 --> 00:10:22.680
and the application of
mRNA goes, of course,

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:26.050
very straight approaches
to develop mRNA vaccines

00:10:26.050 --> 00:10:28.240
against other infectious diseases.

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:29.980
And we are already doing that.

00:10:29.980 --> 00:10:32.270
We have started that early on

00:10:32.270 --> 00:10:36.963
for developing
influenza-based mRNA vaccines.

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:40.020
We are developing mRNA vaccines

00:10:40.020 --> 00:10:43.123
against tuberculosis, against HIV.

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:45.700
BioNTech has also been studying

00:10:45.700 --> 00:10:48.300
the technology in cancer for two decades

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:52.060
and has a cancer vaccine in
Phase II clinical trials.

00:10:52.060 --> 00:10:53.930
They are hoping the cancer field might see

00:10:53.930 --> 00:10:56.270
its first messenger RNA drug approvals

00:10:56.270 --> 00:10:57.683
in two or three years.

00:11:01.710 --> 00:11:04.880
There's no guarantee that
other mRNA vaccines and drugs

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:07.070
will succeed like they have with COVID-19,

00:11:07.070 --> 00:11:10.363
but Karikó remains
undaunted by the challenge.

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:13.490
That's the most important thing,

00:11:13.490 --> 00:11:15.050
to accomplish something

00:11:15.050 --> 00:11:17.389
and, meanwhile, to be happy.

00:11:17.389 --> 00:11:20.060
As long as I was in the lab, I had fun.

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:22.360
It is just such a joy.

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:24.730
Even if things didn't work out

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:26.073
how I expected.

